 most recent change, compare `previous_frame` to `current_frame`, or `last_transition.before_frame` to `last_transition.after_frame`; `history[-1].frame` is the current frame, not the previous one.\nUse Python to inspect the evidence, refine that world model from the newest history, and search or score candidate actions or short sequences against the current goal as you currently understand it.\nMaintain a compact working world model of what the current level seems to contain, what actions appear to do, what the goal seems to be, what is still uncertain, and what plan currently looks best.\nBelow you are provided with the current world model from the previous turn. The default behavior is to copy it and add or remove things based on the evidence that you gathered. BEFORE EXECUTING NEW ACTIONS YOU MUST ALWAYS GIVE THE REVISED VERSION OF THE WORLD MODEL.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it, but stop immediately if a result reports `game_over`, `level_completed`, or `done`.\nend of world model. \nFocus on what changed most recently in `history`, update the target environment change if needed, and separate gameplay-object changes from HUD-only changes.\nWhen ready, call `action(actions)` from inside the `python` tool with the best valid action or ordered batch selected by your code. If your code has found a reliable short sequence, prefer batching it in one call.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it.\nIf you include assistant text before a tool call, keep it short and use it to update the world model. Helpful optional prefixes are `World model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "SPACE caused a major animation with 22 frames. Let me check what changed by comparing before and after frames. The animation involved two objects: a large one (56x64) and the blue block (4x20). Let me inspect the current state.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\nprint(\"Nodes after SPACE:\", len(seg['nodes']))\nfor n in seg['nodes']:\n    print(f\"  id={n['id']} color={n['color']} pixels={n['pixels']} hash={n['hash']} children={n['children']}\")\n    print(f\"    boundary={n['boundary']}\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "Nodes after SPACE: 9\n  id=0 color=N pixels=62 hash=1903ea9b2ee02743 children=[]\n    boundary=[[0, 0], [0, 61]]\n  id=1 color=W pixels=2 hash=7b2d7ac20696a037 children=[]\n    boundary=[[0, 62], [0, 63]]\n  id=2 color=O pixels=3508 hash=6fea84e19c4052a9 children=[5]\n    boundary=[[1, 0], [1, 35], [7, 35], [8, 36], [8, 39], [7, 40], [1, 40], [1, 63], [59, 63], [59, 52], [52, 52], [51, 51], [51, 48], [52, 47], [55, 47], [55, 44], [52, 44], [51, 43], [51, 40], [52, 39], [59, 39], [59, 28], [52, 28], [51, 27], [51, 24], [52, 23], [55, 23], [55, 20], [52, 20], [51, 19], [51, 16], [52, 15], [59, 15], [59, 0]]\n  id=3 color=c pixels=12 hash=cdd21c8b42698580 children=[]\n    boundary=[[1, 36], [1, 39], [3, 39], [3, 36]]\n  id=4 color=M pixels=16 hash=7f085ed05b3bd8d9 children=[]\n    boundary=[[4, 36], [4, 39], [7, 39], [7, 36]]\n  id=5 color=b pixels=80 hash=e2470e6bdc8b22a2 children=[]\n    boundary=[[16, 12], [16, 31], [19, 31], [19, 12]]\n  id=6 color=Y pixels=80 hash=0ea6335cef0189f6 children=[]\n    boundary=[[52, 16], [52, 19], [55, 19], [56, 20], [56, 23], [55, 24], [52, 24], [52, 27], [59, 27], [59, 16]]\n  id=7 color=Y pixels=80 hash=0ea6335cef0189f6 children=[]\n    boundary=[[52, 40], [52, 43], [55, 43], [56, 44], [56, 47], [55, 48], [52, 48], [52, 51], [59, 51], [59, 40]]\n  id=8 color=w pixels=256 hash=3c1ec5f804379565 children=[]\n    boundary=[[60, 0], [60, 63], [63, 63], [63, 0]]", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "Interesting! After pressing SPACE:\n- The top green strip (N) shrank from 64 pixels to 62 pixels, and now there's a white (W) 2-pixel segment at the end of row 0. This looks like a timer/progress bar.\n- All the gameplay objects (charcoal, magenta, blue, yellow, gray) stayed in the same positions with the same pixel counts.\n- The orange background is unchanged.\nSo SPACE just advanced the timer bar by 2 cells. This is a HUD change, not a gameplay change. But the result said `gameplay_changed: True`. Let me check more carefully...\nWait, looking at the animation metadata: 22 frames, 706 total cells changed. The objects were:\n1. A large 56x64 object (624 cells) - this is probably the orange background being redrawn\n2. The blue block (80 cells) - it changed in frame 0 and frame 21\nBut the final state shows the blue block in the same position. The animation might have been the blue block moving and returning, or some other transient effect.\nActually, looking more carefully, the key change is just the top bar shrinking from 64 to 62 (N) with 2 white cells appearing. This is almost certainly a timer/progress bar.\nSince SPACE only advances the timer and doesn't change gameplay, I need to try other actions. Let me try the movement keys (UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT) to see if they move something.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Let me check what the top bar loo